---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import Questions from "@daviddef/archive-kit/components/Questions.astro";
import { u } from "../lib/url.js";
import { md } from "../lib/md.js";
import raw from "../data/questions.json";

/* What this archive does not know, and what would settle each one.
   The kit takes every field as HTML the archive has already rendered, so each
   goes through md() here. STATES ARE THIS ARCHIVE'S OWN THREE:
     open      nobody has answered it yet
     blocked   a person has to do something a machine cannot
     answered  settled, and left on the page so the reasoning stays visible */
const rows = raw.map((r, i) => ({
  ...r, n: i + 1,
  q: md(r.q, u), rank: md(r.rank, u), known: md(r.known, u), settles: md(r.settles, u),
}));
const open = raw.filter((r) => r.state === "open").length;
const blocked = raw.filter((r) => r.state === "blocked").length;
---

<Base title="Open Questions"
      eyebrow="What is not known, and what would settle it"
      dek={`${raw.length} questions on the record — ${open} open, ${blocked} waiting on a person rather than a record, and the rest answered and left standing so the reasoning that settled them stays readable.`}>
  <p class="lede">What this archive does not know, why it does not know it, and — where it matters — what
  it would take to find out. Published because the person holding the missing piece is more likely to be
  a stranger than the person who wrote this.</p>

  <h2>The questions, and what would settle each one</h2>
  <p>Filter by state. <strong>Blocked</strong> means a person has to do something a machine cannot —
  order a file, walk into a library, write a letter. <strong>Answered</strong> ones are left here on
  purpose: the reasoning that settled a question is worth as much as the answer, and two of them were
  settled by overturning what this page used to say.</p>
  <Questions rows={rows} u={u} unit="questions" />

  <h2>Where the searching has already failed</h2>
  <p>Recorded so that the next attempt does not repeat it. Matteo Falco's marriage was searched across
  2,056 transcribed marriage and death pages, including 173 carrying 1750s Latin date-words and 58
  carrying 1760s ones. Filomena Annecchino's birth was searched across 681 harvested Annecchino records.
  Angelo Zampiello's birth was searched to the limit of FamilySearch's deep-paging cap in both
  directions. <strong>Trove is a dead end for this family</strong> — its newspapers largely stop at 1955,
  before these people arrived in Australia.</p>

  <h2>If you are a Falco</h2>
  <p>There are other Arienzo Falco households in these registers that have not been joined to this
  line — Francesco &amp; Mariangela Iuliuccio, Giuseppe &amp; Gelsumina Vigliotta, Antonio &amp;
  Carmela Guida, Vincenzo &amp; Angela Pellone, Angelo &amp; Maria Cimmino, Nicola &amp; Carmela
  Terracciano. Several are probably cousins. If one of them is yours, the
  <a href={u("/households/")}>households</a> are the place to start, and a correction is the most
  valuable thing you can send.</p>
</Base>
